---
import Guide from "../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import CodeGroup from "../../components/CodeGroup.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import LayerStack from "../../components/LayerStack.astro"
import TokenTiers from "../../components/TokenTiers.astro"
import UnreleasedNote from "../../components/UnreleasedNote.astro"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Concepts</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    The architecture: cascade layers, token tiers, scopes, props and classes,
    naming, rich text, and where JavaScript is still needed.
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <h2 id="cascade-layers">Cascade layers</h2>
    <p>
      Every rule lives in a layer, ordered once in <code>layers.css</code>:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@layer openprops, theme, normalize, components.prose, components.root, components.extended, components.compat, utils;
@layer theme.palette, theme.base, theme.custom;`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <LayerStack />
    <p>
      Your theme goes in <code>theme.custom</code>, your overrides unlayered or
      in a layer after <code>utils</code>, so they win without <code
        >!important</code
      > or heavier selectors. <code>components.prose</code> is the lowest component
      layer, so a component inside rich text keeps its own styles. <code
        >components.compat</code
      > is the highest, so a <DocLink href="/guide/browser-support#compat"
        >browser fallback</DocLink
      > beats the component rule it stands in for. Import <code>layers.css</code
      > first when you import single files. Where your CSS goes: <DocLink
        href="/guide/customizing#your-theme">Your theme</DocLink
      > and <DocLink href="/guide/customizing#unlayered-or-your-own-layer"
        >Unlayered or your own layer</DocLink
      >.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="token-tiers">Token tiers</h2>
    <p>
      Each tier reads from the ones before it. A theme sets knobs, roles and
      component tokens, and component CSS starts reading at the roles. <DocLink
        href="/guide/theme-tokens">Theme tokens</DocLink
      > and <code>tokens.json</code> name the tier of every token:
    </p>
    <TokenTiers />
    <p>
      <strong>Knobs</strong> like <code>--ui-palette-hue</code> and <code
        >--ui-density</code
      > are the inputs. <strong>Primitives</strong>, the palette steps <code
        >--ui-color-1</code
      > to <code>--ui-color-16</code> and the type scale <code
        >--ui-font-size-100</code
      > to <code>--ui-font-size-900</code>, derive from them, and component CSS
      never reads them. <strong>Roles</strong>
      such as <code>--ui-primary</code> and <code>--ui-space-3</code> are the contract
      every component reads, each listed on <DocLink href="/guide/theme-tokens"
        >Theme tokens</DocLink
      > with the contrast it promises; a few are hooks, such as <code
        >--ui-control-accent</code
      > and <code>--ui-selected</code>, unset until a theme sets one value for
      every component with that job. Roles plus a few component tokens recreate
      Bootstrap, Material and shadcn in the <DocLink
        href="/guide/customizing#example-themes">example themes</DocLink
      >, and a theme that has to reach into component CSS points at a missing
      role.
      <strong>Component tokens</strong> like <code>--ui-button-size</code> cover a
      job only one component has. <strong>Private properties</strong> like <code
        >--_accent</code
      > are one component's wiring for one element, see <DocLink
        href="/guide/customizing#component-properties"
        >Component properties</DocLink
      >. The documented inputs among them are per instance: the component
      declares them on itself in <code>components.root</code>, so a theme in <code
        >theme.custom</code
      > can't set them site-wide. <DocLink href="/guide/theming"
        >Theming</DocLink
      > walks through the knobs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="light-and-dark">Light and dark</h2>
    <p>
      Every color token holds both schemes in one <code>light-dark()</code> under
      <code>color-scheme: light dark</code>, so the OS setting drives them and
      there is no class to toggle. <code>.ui-light</code> and <code
        >.ui-dark</code
      > pick a side for <code>html</code> or one element, see <DocLink
        href="/guide/theming#scopes">Scopes</DocLink
      >.
    </p>
    <Example>
      <div class="concepts-schemes">
        <div class="ui-light">
          <button class="ui-button ui-filled ui-primary" type="button">
            Always light
          </button>
        </div>
        <div class="ui-dark">
          <button class="ui-button ui-filled ui-primary" type="button">
            Always dark
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Example>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="palette-scopes">Palette scopes</h2>
    <p>
      Every color comes from one 16-step palette. A severity class re-derives it
      from the intent color for an element and everything inside, so one class
      gives a Callout and a tonal Button the same red, while <code
        >--ui-primary</code
      > and the text colors keep the page values. <code>.ui-palette</code> does the
      same from your own knobs. The classes and a live demo are on <DocLink
        href="/guide/theming#scopes">Theming</DocLink
      >.
    </p>
    <figure class="concepts-palettes ui-not-rich-text">
      {
        [
          "",
          "ui-critical",
          "ui-info",
          "ui-neutral",
          "ui-success",
          "ui-warning",
        ].map((scope) => (
          <div class={scope || undefined}>
            <code>{scope ? `.${scope}` : "Page"}</code>
            <span class="swatches" aria-hidden="true">
              {Array.from({ length: 16 }, (_, i) => (
                <span style={`background-color: var(--ui-color-${i + 1})`} />
              ))}
            </span>
          </div>
        ))
      }
      <figcaption>
        <code>--ui-color-1</code> to <code>--ui-color-16</code> on the page and in
        each scope, live with the current theme.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="props-and-classes">Props and classes</h2>
    <p>
      A component is one <code>ui-</code> class on a native element, with modifier
      classes for its options. The Astro, React, Svelte and Vue components render
      those exact classes from props, one to one:
    </p>
    <CodeGroup
      tabs={[
        {
          title: "HTML",
          code: `<button class="ui-button ui-outlined ui-small ui-primary" type="button">
  Save
</button>`,
          lang: "html",
        },
        {
          title: "Astro",
          code: `<Button variant="outlined" size="small" color="primary">Save</Button>`,
          lang: "astro",
        },
        {
          title: "React",
          code: `<Button variant="outlined" size="small" color="primary">
  Save
</Button>`,
          lang: "tsx",
        },
        {
          title: "Svelte",
          code: `<Button variant="outlined" size="small" color="primary">Save</Button>`,
          lang: "svelte",
        },
        {
          title: "Vue",
          code: `<Button variant="outlined" size="small" color="primary">Save</Button>`,
          lang: "vue",
        },
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      Boolean props become a class of the same name (<code>round</code> is <code
        >.ui-round</code
      >), and native states stay native attributes, like <code>disabled</code>.
      The API tables list prop and class side by side.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="naming">Naming</h2>
    <p>
      One idea has one prop name and one set of values in every component, so
      what you learn on one page carries over to the next.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Idea</th>
          <th>Prop</th>
          <th>Values</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Paint only</td>
          <td><code>color</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>critical</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>neutral</code>, <code
              >primary</code
            >, <code>success</code>, <code>warning</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Meaning</td>
          <td><code>severity</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>critical</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>neutral</code>, <code
              >success</code
            >, <code>warning</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Surface</td>
          <td><code>variant</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>elevated</code>, <code>filled</code>, <code>line</code>, <code
              >outlined</code
            >, <code>plain</code>, <code>tonal</code>, <code>transparent</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Size</td>
          <td><code>size</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>x-small</code>, <code>small</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code
              >large</code
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Density</td>
          <td><code>dense</code> or <code>density</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>dense</code>, <code>normal</code>, <code>spacious</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Shape</td>
          <td><code>round</code> or <code>shape</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>circle</code>, <code>rounded</code>, <code>squared</code>, <code
              >squircle</code
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Layout axis</td>
          <td><code>orientation</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>auto</code>, <code>horizontal</code>, <code>vertical</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Side of a trigger or the screen</td>
          <td><code>side</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>block-end</code>, <code>block-start</code>, <code
              >inline-end</code
            >, <code>inline-start</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Alignment on that side or in a row</td>
          <td><code>align</code></td>
          <td><code>start</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>end</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Corner</td>
          <td><code>corner</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>start-start</code>, <code>start-end</code>, <code
              >end-start</code
            >, <code>end-end</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Any CSS position</td>
          <td><code>positionArea</code></td>
          <td>any <code>position-area</code> value</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Checked state</td>
          <td><code>checked</code></td>
          <td>the native attribute</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A component takes the values it supports from its set. <code>color</code> only
      changes the paint. <code>severity</code> says what something means and adds
      what that meaning needs, like the icon of a <DocLink
        href="/components/callout#severities">Callout</DocLink
      > or the shaped dot of a <DocLink href="/components/chip#severities"
        >Chip</DocLink
      >, because a color alone doesn't tell everyone.
    </p>

    <h3 id="variants">Variants</h3>
    <p>
      A variant describes the resting look. A hover or a selected tint never
      counts against it. <code>filled</code> is a solid fill in the component's own
      color: the accent on a Button, a surface on a field or a track. <code
        >tonal</code
      > is the tonal surface, <code>outlined</code> a border with no fill, <code
        >elevated</code
      > a raised surface with a shadow, <code>transparent</code> no surface and no
      border at rest, and <code>line</code> an indicator with no track, on <DocLink
        href="/components/tabs#line">Tabs</DocLink
      >. <code>plain</code> is the page surface with no border, and only differs from
      <code>transparent</code> on a tinted background:
    </p>
    <Example centered>
      <div class="concepts-ladder">
        {
          ["page", "tonal"].map((surface) => (
            <div
              class:list={[
                "concepts-surface",
                { "is-tonal": surface === "tonal" },
              ]}
            >
              <span class="concepts-surface-label">
                {surface === "tonal" ? "On a tonal surface" : "On the page"}
              </span>
              {["plain", "transparent", "tonal", "outlined", "elevated"].map(
                (variant) => (
                  <div
                    class:list={[
                      "ui-card",
                      variant !== "plain" && `ui-${variant}`,
                    ]}
                  >
                    <div class="ui-content">
                      <code>{variant}</code>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                ),
              )}
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </Example>
    <p>
      Every enum names its default, which adds no class: <code
        >size="medium"</code
      >, <code>variant="transparent"</code> on a Button, <code
        >variant="plain"</code
      > on a Card. Leaving the prop out and writing the default give the same result.
      <code>color</code> and <code>severity</code> have no default: leaving them out
      means none.
    </p>

    <h3 id="start-and-end">Start and end</h3>
    <p>
      <code>start</code> and <code>end</code> follow the writing direction along the
      axis that fits: <code>.ui-start</code> and <code>.ui-end</code> hold content
      at the start and end of a row, and <code>startText</code> and <code
        >endText</code
      > put text above and below a field.
    </p>

    <h3 id="checked">Checked</h3>
    <p>
      A <DocLink href="/components/toggle">ToggleButton</DocLink> or a <DocLink
        href="/components/tabs">TabsItem</DocLink
      > takes <code>checked</code>, the attribute it sets on its input, with
      each framework's own rules. In React <code>checked</code> with <code
        >onChange</code
      > controls it and <code>defaultChecked</code> sets where it starts. In a group
      that owns the value, such as a ToggleGroup with a <code>value</code>, the
      group wins.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="rich-text">Rich text</h2>
    <p>
      <code>.ui-rich-text</code> styles the classless elements inside it, for Markdown
      or CMS output. Elements with a class are left alone, so components keep their
      styles, and <code>.ui-not-rich-text</code> opts a subtree out.
    </p>
    <Example>
      <article class="ui-rich-text">
        <h3>Release notes</h3>
        <p>Plain HTML, styled by the wrapper.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>No classes needed</li>
          <li>Components keep their own styles</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="ui-not-rich-text">
          <ul>
            <li>This list is left alone</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </article>
    </Example>
    <p>
      <code>.ui-h1</code> to <code>.ui-h6</code> give the heading styles anywhere
      else. See <DocLink href="/components/typography">Typography</DocLink>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="javascript">JavaScript</h2>
    <p>These work without JavaScript:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Accordion is a <code>details</code> element, Tabs are a radio group, and ToggleButton
        and ToggleGroup are checkboxes and radios.
      </li>
      <li>
        Dialog, Drawer and Menu open with Invoker Commands (<code>command</code> and
        <code>commandfor</code> on a button) and the Popover API.
      </li>
      <li>
        Tooltip and hover Anchor show with <code>interestfor</code> and CSS anchor
        positioning.
      </li>
      <li>
        Carousel buttons and markers are generated by CSS, and the Range track
        fill is a scroll-driven animation.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Some are fresh off the press, see <DocLink href="/guide/browser-support"
        >Browser support</DocLink
      > for the fallbacks. Two things need script in plain HTML:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Indeterminate checkboxes</strong>: <code>indeterminate</code> is a
        property, not an attribute. Mark the input with <code
          >data-indeterminate</code
        > and call <code>activateIndeterminate()</code> from <code
          >css/js/checkbox.js</code
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Range value</strong>: the <code>output</code> has to be updated on
        <code>input</code>. Mark it with <code>data-live</code> and call <code
          >initRange()</code
        > from <code>css/js/range.js</code>. The Astro component does that for
        you, and React, Svelte and Vue update it reactively.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <CodeGroup
      tabs={[
        {
          title: "CDN",
          code: `<script type="module">
  import { activateIndeterminate } from "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/opui-css@6/css/js/checkbox.js"
  import { initRange } from "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/opui-css@6/css/js/range.js"

  activateIndeterminate()
  initRange()
</script>`,
          lang: "html",
        },
        {
          title: "Bundler",
          code: `<script type="module">
  import { activateIndeterminate } from "opui-css/css/js/checkbox.js"
  import { initRange } from "opui-css/css/js/range.js"

  activateIndeterminate()
  initRange()
</script>`,
          lang: "html",
        },
      ]}
    />
    <UnreleasedNote file="css/js/" />
  </section>
</Guide>

<style>
  .concepts-palettes {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--size-2);
    margin-block: var(--size-5);
    place-items: stretch;

    > div {
      align-items: center;
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--size-3);
      grid-template-columns: 7rem 1fr;
    }

    code {
      font-size: var(--ui-font-size-small);
    }

    figcaption {
      color: var(--ui-text);
      font-size: var(--ui-font-size-small);
      margin-block-start: var(--size-1);
      text-align: start;
    }
  }

  .swatches {
    border: var(--ui-border-width) solid currentColor;
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-small);
    display: grid;
    forced-color-adjust: none;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(16, 1fr);
    overflow: hidden;

    > span {
      block-size: var(--size-6);
    }
  }

  .concepts-ladder {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--size-3);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
    inline-size: 100%;
  }

  .concepts-surface {
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius);
    display: grid;
    grid-row: span 6;
    grid-template-rows: subgrid;
    padding: var(--size-3);

    &.is-tonal {
      background-color: var(--ui-surface-tonal);
    }
  }

  .concepts-surface-label {
    color: var(--ui-text);
    font-size: var(--ui-font-size-small);
  }

  .concepts-schemes {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--size-3);

    > div {
      border-radius: var(--ui-radius);
      padding: var(--size-4);
    }
  }
</style>
